    ],
    "intro": "French beans are tender summer beans, grown for crisp young pods, fresh shelled flageolets or fully ripened dried haricots. They are far less hardy than broad beans and should not be rushed into cold soil, but once the weather is warm they are quick, colourful and productive. Dwarf types crop earliest and need no formal supports, although their low pods can be dirtied by rain or damaged by slugs. Climbing types take more work to set up, but they keep the pods off the soil, reduce bending at picking time and can crop for longer.",
